<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  macOS entitlements. Deliberately not a copy of the iOS file.

	  Absent by design:

	  - NFC. CoreNFC does not exist on macOS and the reader-session format
	    would fail provisioning.
	  - iCloud key-value storage. Everything below is grantable by an ad-hoc
	    signature, which is what lets Scripts/build-macos.sh produce a running
	    app with no Apple Developer setup at all. `ubiquity-kvstore-identifier`
	    is the one entitlement here that requires a real provisioning profile,
	    so including it by default would break that. To restore iCloud seed
	    backup on a signed build, add:

	        <key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
	        <string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>

	    and build with DEV_TEAM=... Without it, NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore
	    reads and writes are dropped and backup falls back to local only.

	  Present by design:

	  - The sandbox is on, so outbound network access must be requested
	    explicitly. Without `network.client` every mint and relay call fails,
	    which is the single most confusing way for this app to break.
	  - Camera, for scanning QR codes with the built-in webcam.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
